# Post-merge checklist — canonical instruction

What a run leaves behind once its PR is merged. Both `/goal:auto` (final report) and
`/goal:next` (when no unchecked iteration remains) emit THIS text, filled from the plan.
Single source of truth — edit here, both follow.

The run itself never performs any of it. `/goal:auto` may not delete a branch, a tag or a
label, so every line below is the developer's to run. The point of printing it is that the
merge happens hours or days after the run ends, in a session that has no memory of it:
whatever is not written down at that moment is what gets forgotten.

## How to fill it

- `<default>` = the repository's default branch (`main` unless the plan says otherwise).
- `<branch>` = the run's branch, one line per branch when the plan had parallel tracks.
- `<work-id>` = the plan's `Work-id:` header.
- `<backup refs>` = any tag or branch created as a safety net during the run; drop the
  line when there is none.
- `<issue>` = the backing issue number; drop lines 5 and 6 when no issue backs the work.
- `<follow-up>` = `.claude/plans/<work-id>-tracks-spec.md` or `-cleanup-spec.md` when the
  plan named one; drop the line when it did not.

## The block

```text
The PR is merged. What that leaves behind, none of which the run may do for you:

1. Update <default> locally — git fetch --prune, then fast-forward.
2. Delete <branch>, local AND remote. Verify by CONTENT, not ancestry: a squash-merge
   leaves the branch looking unmerged to `git branch --merged` while its work is on
   <default>. Check a file the PR added is present there.
3. Delete <backup refs> — the rebase net, useless once the merge holds.
4. Reconcile .claude/plans/<work-id>-spec.md: its checkboxes still carry the placeholder
   label the plan was written with. Say what landed, with the PR number and merge commit.
5. Clear the run controls on issue #<issue> — `goal:no-ship`, `goal:stop`,
   `goal:skip-lenses`. They persist across runs, and a stale one silently blocks the next.
6. Close issue #<issue> if every PR from this plan is merged. One plan can produce
   several, which is why the PR bodies say `Refs`, never `Closes`.
7. <follow-up> — its Trigger now holds. Run the workflow on it when you want it.
```

## Rules

- **Print it, never execute it.** Deleting branches, tags and labels is outside what any
  goal command is allowed to do, and the developer may have reasons to keep any of them.
- **Drop the lines that do not apply** rather than emit them with an empty placeholder. A
  checklist carrying `<follow-up>` unfilled teaches the reader to skim it.
- **Never assert a branch is merged from `git branch --merged` alone.** Under squash-merge
  it is wrong, and a wrong deletion here loses unpushed work.
